{"name":"Apple Orchard","hex":"#93c96a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"apple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, firm, round fruit produced by a tree of the genus Malus.","The fruit of the tree Malus domestica, chiefly with a green, red, or yellow skin, cultivated in temperate climates for cidermaking, cooking, and eating. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make (something) appear like an apple (noun sense 1.1).","To become like an apple.\n\n To collect fir-cones.\n Of a flower bud or vegetable (especially a root vegetable): to grow into the shape of an apple."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apple"},{"term":"orchard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A garden or an area of land for the cultivation of fruit or nut trees.","The trees themselves cultivated in such an area."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orchard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Orchard","blocks":[{"text":"An orchard is an intentional plantation of trees or shrubs that is maintained for food production. Orchards comprise fruit- or nut-producing trees that are generally grown for commercial production. Orchards are also sometimes a feature of large gardens, where they serve an aesthetic as well as a productive purpose. A fruit garden is generally synonymous with an orchard, although it is set on a smaller, non-commercial scale and may emphasize berry shrubs in preference to fruit trees. Most temperate-zone orchards are laid out in a regular grid, with a grazed or mown grass or bare soil base that makes maintenance and fruit gathering easy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Orchard_Lanes.jpg/330px-Orchard_Lanes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchard","wikidata":"Q236371"},{"title":"Apple","blocks":[{"text":"An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree. Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple, the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple","wikidata":"Q89"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg/500px-Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg/1280px-Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg/500px-Streuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meadow orchard (Streuobstwiese) with view to the Lochenhörnle [de]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStreuobstwiesen%2C_Blick_auf_Traufgang_und_Lochenh%C3%B6rnle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Orchards_in_snow%2C_Sangla%2C_Himachal_Pradesh%2C_India.jpg/500px-Orchards_in_snow%2C_Sangla%2C_Himachal_Pradesh%2C_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co